WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.490 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:00.490 --> 00:00:00.990 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:00.990 --> 00:00:04.410 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:04.410 --> 00:00:12.560 align:middle line:90%


00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:14.900 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: All right,
we'll get started.

00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:16.940 align:middle line:84%
We're going to
continue our discussion

00:00:16.940 --> 00:00:19.440 align:middle line:84%
of principal
components analysis.

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:24.560 align:middle line:84%
And what I want to emphasize
with principal components

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:31.550 align:middle line:84%
is that we're dealing with
a random vector X that's

00:00:31.550 --> 00:00:35.120 align:middle line:90%
in m dimensional space.

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:42.720 align:middle line:84%
And it has an expected
value of alpha, say.

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:53.120 align:middle line:84%
And it has a covariance matrix
given by sigma, which is m by m.

00:00:53.120 --> 00:01:01.120 align:middle line:84%
This kind of setup can be used
for modeling returns on stocks

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:05.410 align:middle line:84%
or assets that may have a
mean return over some holding

00:01:05.410 --> 00:01:09.520 align:middle line:84%
period and a
variance-covariance matrix.

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:15.680 align:middle line:84%
And so in working
with financial data,

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:20.200 align:middle line:84%
principal components
analysis can be quite useful.

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:23.950 align:middle line:84%
Essentially, what
principal components does

00:01:23.950 --> 00:01:32.140 align:middle line:84%
is it transforms
our X vector to a p

00:01:32.140 --> 00:01:35.120 align:middle line:84%
vector of principal
component variables.

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:40.930 align:middle line:84%
And the definitions of
these is we subtract

00:01:40.930 --> 00:01:45.100 align:middle line:90%
from X the mean vector.

00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:52.810 align:middle line:84%
And then we rotate the
coordinate axes of this m vector

00:01:52.810 --> 00:02:02.530 align:middle line:90%
by this gamma transpose matrix.

00:02:02.530 --> 00:02:10.889 align:middle line:84%
And so this gamma
transpose can equal gamma

00:02:10.889 --> 00:02:16.710 align:middle line:84%
1 transpose, gamma 2 transpose,
down to gamma M transpose.

00:02:16.710 --> 00:02:21.360 align:middle line:90%
And so the first component--

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:24.220 align:middle line:90%
p1 down to pm.

00:02:24.220 --> 00:02:31.690 align:middle line:84%
The first component corresponds
to using this gamma vector,

00:02:31.690 --> 00:02:35.790 align:middle line:84%
which happens to
be an eigenvector

00:02:35.790 --> 00:02:41.670 align:middle line:84%
of the covariance matrix
corresponding to where

00:02:41.670 --> 00:02:45.090 align:middle line:84%
with associated
eigenvalue lambda i.

00:02:45.090 --> 00:02:55.140 align:middle line:84%
And in this representation, we
basically have sigma gamma i

00:02:55.140 --> 00:03:00.130 align:middle line:90%
is equal to lambda i gamma i.

00:03:00.130 --> 00:03:12.520 align:middle line:84%
And we can order our eigenvalues
from largest to smallest.

00:03:12.520 --> 00:03:17.440 align:middle line:84%
And the first principal
component variable

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:21.850 align:middle line:90%
corresponds to lining up--

00:03:21.850 --> 00:03:23.830 align:middle line:84%
or looking at a
coordinate system where

00:03:23.830 --> 00:03:26.440 align:middle line:84%
we're looking along the
dimension of greatest

00:03:26.440 --> 00:03:28.700 align:middle line:90%
variability in the data.

00:03:28.700 --> 00:03:35.620 align:middle line:84%
So this covariance
structure it's

00:03:35.620 --> 00:03:41.290 align:middle line:84%
actually useful to think
of a special example, which

00:03:41.290 --> 00:03:45.130 align:middle line:84%
is the multivariate
normal distribution in m

00:03:45.130 --> 00:03:49.990 align:middle line:84%
dimensions with mean vector
alpha and covariance matrix

00:03:49.990 --> 00:03:51.850 align:middle line:90%
sigma.

00:03:51.850 --> 00:03:57.700 align:middle line:84%
And so if we think
of X1 and x2--

00:03:57.700 --> 00:04:00.840 align:middle line:84%
we'll just consider a
two-dimensional case

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:02.440 align:middle line:90%
as an example.

00:04:02.440 --> 00:04:10.120 align:middle line:84%
There could be an alpha 1,
alpha 2 here for the mean of X1,

00:04:10.120 --> 00:04:12.780 align:middle line:90%
first component mean of X2.

00:04:12.780 --> 00:04:20.430 align:middle line:84%
And with a bivariate
normal distribution,

00:04:20.430 --> 00:04:26.160 align:middle line:84%
we basically will have
contours of the density,

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:31.530 align:middle line:84%
the joint density,
of X1 and X2 that

00:04:31.530 --> 00:04:37.870 align:middle line:84%
are given by ellipses
centered at the mean vector.

00:04:37.870 --> 00:04:42.540 align:middle line:84%
And the degree to which
the components are highly

00:04:42.540 --> 00:04:43.720 align:middle line:90%
correlated--

00:04:43.720 --> 00:04:45.930 align:middle line:84%
if it's a positive
correlation, then we

00:04:45.930 --> 00:04:49.990 align:middle line:84%
have ellipses that
are tilted up.

00:04:49.990 --> 00:04:53.080 align:middle line:84%
If it's a negative correlation,
it would be tilted down.

00:04:53.080 --> 00:05:01.400 align:middle line:84%
Now, what principal components
analysis does is it basically--

00:05:01.400 --> 00:05:06.140 align:middle line:84%
this step here
corresponds to shifting

00:05:06.140 --> 00:05:11.730 align:middle line:84%
the coordinate axes to,
say, x1 star and x2 star.

00:05:11.730 --> 00:05:17.900 align:middle line:84%
So we recenter our space at
the point with the origin

00:05:17.900 --> 00:05:21.180 align:middle line:84%
being at the mean
point of the outcomes.

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:29.390 align:middle line:84%
And then we consider rotating
these axes in such a way

00:05:29.390 --> 00:05:36.740 align:middle line:84%
that the first principal
component axis corresponding

00:05:36.740 --> 00:05:43.010 align:middle line:84%
to the largest eigenvalue,
it will orient along

00:05:43.010 --> 00:05:47.540 align:middle line:84%
the principal axis
of the ellipse.

00:05:47.540 --> 00:05:53.230 align:middle line:84%
And the second
principal component axis

00:05:53.230 --> 00:05:58.090 align:middle line:90%
will be orthogonal to the first.

00:05:58.090 --> 00:06:02.830 align:middle line:84%
And it will-- well,
in two dimensions

00:06:02.830 --> 00:06:06.700 align:middle line:84%
it will have variability
along that dimension that's

00:06:06.700 --> 00:06:08.690 align:middle line:90%
smaller than P1.

00:06:08.690 --> 00:06:12.640 align:middle line:84%
When we extend from a
two-dimensional case

00:06:12.640 --> 00:06:15.430 align:middle line:90%
to an m-dimensional case--

00:06:15.430 --> 00:06:20.680 align:middle line:84%
so suppose we have
500 stocks or 1,000,

00:06:20.680 --> 00:06:26.120 align:middle line:84%
then if we apply a multinomial
model to that case,

00:06:26.120 --> 00:06:31.750 align:middle line:84%
then our principal components
will be identifying

00:06:31.750 --> 00:06:33.850 align:middle line:90%
coordinates--

00:06:33.850 --> 00:06:37.510 align:middle line:84%
basically coordinate
system variables

00:06:37.510 --> 00:06:43.300 align:middle line:84%
that line up with different
orthogonal dimensions

00:06:43.300 --> 00:06:45.260 align:middle line:90%
of variability.

00:06:45.260 --> 00:06:52.200 align:middle line:84%
And so what ends up being very
useful is that, at least when we

00:06:52.200 --> 00:06:58.740 align:middle line:84%
look at a market of stocks or
asset returns, some of those

00:06:58.740 --> 00:07:01.680 align:middle line:84%
may be highly correlated
with each other.

00:07:01.680 --> 00:07:05.640 align:middle line:84%
Others may be, in a sense,
orthogonal or uncorrelated

00:07:05.640 --> 00:07:06.960 align:middle line:90%
with each other.

00:07:06.960 --> 00:07:09.790 align:middle line:84%
And with principal
components analysis,

00:07:09.790 --> 00:07:17.040 align:middle line:84%
we can actually identify
different directions

00:07:17.040 --> 00:07:21.630 align:middle line:84%
in the space, which
basically have

00:07:21.630 --> 00:07:24.610 align:middle line:84%
decreasing levels
of variability,

00:07:24.610 --> 00:07:28.950 align:middle line:84%
but that are also orthogonal
to each other or uncorrelated

00:07:28.950 --> 00:07:30.220 align:middle line:90%
with each other.

00:07:30.220 --> 00:07:36.790 align:middle line:84%
So when we want to specify
factors, underlying factors,

00:07:36.790 --> 00:07:40.870 align:middle line:84%
say, in joint distributions
of returns and markets,

00:07:40.870 --> 00:07:43.650 align:middle line:84%
principal components can
be very helpful in trying

00:07:43.650 --> 00:07:46.000 align:middle line:90%
to identify those.

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:49.500 align:middle line:84%
And later on, we'll talk
about factor modeling

00:07:49.500 --> 00:07:54.800 align:middle line:84%
and see how close or how
effective principal components

00:07:54.800 --> 00:07:56.270 align:middle line:90%
can be.

00:07:56.270 --> 00:08:00.530 align:middle line:84%
But there are also
general factor models

00:08:00.530 --> 00:08:03.410 align:middle line:90%
that we'll introduce later.

00:08:03.410 --> 00:08:09.320 align:middle line:84%
So with these transformations,
as noted here,

00:08:09.320 --> 00:08:12.080 align:middle line:84%
these principal
component variables

00:08:12.080 --> 00:08:18.950 align:middle line:84%
have basically a mean that's
zero and a covariance matrix

00:08:18.950 --> 00:08:21.530 align:middle line:90%
that's diagonal.

00:08:21.530 --> 00:08:24.290 align:middle line:84%
So these principal
component variables

00:08:24.290 --> 00:08:26.640 align:middle line:84%
are uncorrelated
with each other.

00:08:26.640 --> 00:08:31.650 align:middle line:84%
And if we order the eigenvalues
from largest to smallest,

00:08:31.650 --> 00:08:34.520 align:middle line:84%
the first principal
component variable

00:08:34.520 --> 00:08:39.539 align:middle line:84%
will be the axis with
the greatest variability.

00:08:39.539 --> 00:08:42.740 align:middle line:84%
Now, what's interesting
to highlight

00:08:42.740 --> 00:08:48.020 align:middle line:84%
is that in the transformation
from the original X's

00:08:48.020 --> 00:08:50.530 align:middle line:84%
to the principal
component variables,

00:08:50.530 --> 00:08:55.020 align:middle line:84%
the only thing we've
done is shift the origin

00:08:55.020 --> 00:09:02.590 align:middle line:84%
and then rotated the coordinate
axes in an orthogonal way.

00:09:02.590 --> 00:09:08.070 align:middle line:84%
So we're maintaining right
angles between coordinate axes.

00:09:08.070 --> 00:09:09.790 align:middle line:90%
And that's all we've done.

00:09:09.790 --> 00:09:16.890 align:middle line:90%
So if we have outcomes of X--

00:09:16.890 --> 00:09:21.130 align:middle line:84%
so if we have X is
a random variable,

00:09:21.130 --> 00:09:24.630 align:middle line:84%
but then we may
have realizations--

00:09:24.630 --> 00:09:28.470 align:middle line:90%
realizations, say, X--

00:09:28.470 --> 00:09:31.140 align:middle line:90%
I don't know-- a, Xb.

00:09:31.140 --> 00:09:35.040 align:middle line:84%
I don't want to have
integers referring

00:09:35.040 --> 00:09:38.290 align:middle line:84%
to these because I don't want
to confuse the components.

00:09:38.290 --> 00:09:43.620 align:middle line:84%
But we could have different
realizations for different days

00:09:43.620 --> 00:09:46.650 align:middle line:84%
or different analysis
periods, and we

00:09:46.650 --> 00:09:50.630 align:middle line:84%
would get different
points of realizations

00:09:50.630 --> 00:09:53.460 align:middle line:90%
of the outcomes of this vector.

00:09:53.460 --> 00:09:58.160 align:middle line:84%
And so principal
components allows us just

00:09:58.160 --> 00:10:05.240 align:middle line:84%
to view the data in a way
that hasn't transformed

00:10:05.240 --> 00:10:07.980 align:middle line:90%
the data in an unusual way.

00:10:07.980 --> 00:10:10.250 align:middle line:84%
Basically, the
configuration of points

00:10:10.250 --> 00:10:16.670 align:middle line:84%
is the same before and after
the construction of these PC

00:10:16.670 --> 00:10:17.760 align:middle line:90%
variables.

00:10:17.760 --> 00:10:21.320 align:middle line:90%
So let's see.

00:10:21.320 --> 00:10:26.750 align:middle line:84%
OK, there's this version
of principal components

00:10:26.750 --> 00:10:32.390 align:middle line:84%
where we consider splitting the
first k principal components

00:10:32.390 --> 00:10:40.010 align:middle line:84%
into a gamma 1 matrix and the
remaining ones into a gamma 2

00:10:40.010 --> 00:10:41.220 align:middle line:90%
portion.

00:10:41.220 --> 00:10:44.390 align:middle line:84%
And so one can
then think of there

00:10:44.390 --> 00:10:48.370 align:middle line:84%
being a linear model
for our outcome

00:10:48.370 --> 00:10:56.230 align:middle line:84%
x variable, which might be
simplified to just a mean vector

00:10:56.230 --> 00:11:04.870 align:middle line:84%
plus a matrix times the
first k principal component

00:11:04.870 --> 00:11:07.720 align:middle line:90%
variables plus error.

00:11:07.720 --> 00:11:14.380 align:middle line:84%
If this, in fact, were
an effective model,

00:11:14.380 --> 00:11:20.200 align:middle line:84%
it might be the case that
the last m minus k principal

00:11:20.200 --> 00:11:24.370 align:middle line:84%
component variables essentially
have no important information

00:11:24.370 --> 00:11:25.480 align:middle line:90%
in them.

00:11:25.480 --> 00:11:33.560 align:middle line:84%
And so this could be a very
simple model for our x variable.

00:11:33.560 --> 00:11:40.840 align:middle line:84%
And importantly, if this
were an appropriate model

00:11:40.840 --> 00:11:48.840 align:middle line:84%
for an m vector x, then the
covariance matrix of the--

00:11:48.840 --> 00:11:57.660 align:middle line:84%
the covariance matrix
of X is just given by--

00:11:57.660 --> 00:11:59.220 align:middle line:90%
or let's see.

00:11:59.220 --> 00:12:03.960 align:middle line:84%
Or the-- yeah, the
covariance matrix of X,

00:12:03.960 --> 00:12:06.630 align:middle line:84%
in terms of the
diagonal entries which

00:12:06.630 --> 00:12:11.580 align:middle line:84%
represent the correlations,
are all due to common loadings

00:12:11.580 --> 00:12:15.480 align:middle line:84%
on these different principal
component variables in p1.

00:12:15.480 --> 00:12:21.930 align:middle line:84%
So the interesting
question to think about is,

00:12:21.930 --> 00:12:30.850 align:middle line:84%
how can we model random vectors
with some covariance matrix?

00:12:30.850 --> 00:12:34.530 align:middle line:84%
And are there simple structures
to the covariance matrix

00:12:34.530 --> 00:12:39.600 align:middle line:84%
that explain all of
the intercorrelations?

00:12:39.600 --> 00:12:48.180 align:middle line:84%
Now, when we perform empirical
principal components analysis,

00:12:48.180 --> 00:12:52.100 align:middle line:90%
then what we do is we--

00:12:52.100 --> 00:12:57.620 align:middle line:84%
say, we have an X
matrix, which has

00:12:57.620 --> 00:13:03.960 align:middle line:84%
columns X1 through X sub capital
T that are all m vectors.

00:13:03.960 --> 00:13:07.060 align:middle line:90%
Then we compute the row means--

00:13:07.060 --> 00:13:09.650 align:middle line:90%


00:13:09.650 --> 00:13:18.120 align:middle line:84%
we subtract those row means
from the matrix row by row.

00:13:18.120 --> 00:13:25.850 align:middle line:84%
And then with this X star matrix
that's the demeaned matrix,

00:13:25.850 --> 00:13:33.890 align:middle line:84%
that matrix times its transpose
divided by the number of columns

00:13:33.890 --> 00:13:37.670 align:middle line:84%
in X capital T,
that is an estimate

00:13:37.670 --> 00:13:40.400 align:middle line:84%
of the covariance
matrix, essentially

00:13:40.400 --> 00:13:42.920 align:middle line:90%
the sample covariance matrix.

00:13:42.920 --> 00:13:48.460 align:middle line:84%
And we then can perform
an eigenvalue eigenvector

00:13:48.460 --> 00:13:51.500 align:middle line:84%
decomposition of this
covariance matrix,

00:13:51.500 --> 00:13:56.440 align:middle line:84%
and then use these
results to define

00:13:56.440 --> 00:13:59.800 align:middle line:84%
the empirical
principal component

00:13:59.800 --> 00:14:02.420 align:middle line:90%
coordinates of our data.

00:14:02.420 --> 00:14:04.510 align:middle line:84%
And it corresponds
to just taking

00:14:04.510 --> 00:14:09.290 align:middle line:84%
our estimate of the gamma matrix
transpose the demeaned matrix.

00:14:09.290 --> 00:14:12.824 align:middle line:84%
So this is very
simple to implement.

00:14:12.824 --> 00:14:16.270 align:middle line:90%


00:14:16.270 --> 00:14:18.260 align:middle line:90%
And let's see.

00:14:18.260 --> 00:14:23.950 align:middle line:84%
The singular value
decomposition is also

00:14:23.950 --> 00:14:28.480 align:middle line:84%
a way of obtaining these
principal component

00:14:28.480 --> 00:14:30.170 align:middle line:90%
transformations.

00:14:30.170 --> 00:14:36.220 align:middle line:84%
If we look at VDU transpose
being the singular value

00:14:36.220 --> 00:14:40.770 align:middle line:84%
decomposition of our X
star demeaned matrix,

00:14:40.770 --> 00:14:49.520 align:middle line:84%
then we have our
lambda estimate just

00:14:49.520 --> 00:14:53.360 align:middle line:84%
being the square of the
D diagonal matrix divided

00:14:53.360 --> 00:14:57.230 align:middle line:84%
by T. The estimate
of the gamma matrix

00:14:57.230 --> 00:15:03.820 align:middle line:84%
is simply the V. And our
principal component variables--

00:15:03.820 --> 00:15:07.610 align:middle line:84%
or our data transformed to
principal component coordinates

00:15:07.610 --> 00:15:11.640 align:middle line:90%
is simply given by DU prime.

00:15:11.640 --> 00:15:17.910 align:middle line:84%
So principal components analysis
is very easy to implement.

00:15:17.910 --> 00:15:21.050 align:middle line:84%
And the lecture
that was originally

00:15:21.050 --> 00:15:24.470 align:middle line:84%
scheduled for today,
Stefan Andreev,

00:15:24.470 --> 00:15:27.440 align:middle line:84%
he's going to go through
a number of examples

00:15:27.440 --> 00:15:31.850 align:middle line:84%
of applying principal
components in equity markets

00:15:31.850 --> 00:15:35.310 align:middle line:90%
and in bond markets.

00:15:35.310 --> 00:15:42.460 align:middle line:84%
What's really useful
to anticipate is that--

00:15:42.460 --> 00:15:45.820 align:middle line:84%
well, one of the examples
that he's used in the past

00:15:45.820 --> 00:15:51.160 align:middle line:84%
is where the X vector
corresponds to index returns

00:15:51.160 --> 00:15:56.140 align:middle line:84%
in different European
markets on a daily basis,

00:15:56.140 --> 00:16:04.690 align:middle line:84%
and attempting to understand
the joint variability across all

00:16:04.690 --> 00:16:08.950 align:middle line:84%
of the markets, but also
to understand where there

00:16:08.950 --> 00:16:15.700 align:middle line:84%
are different geographical
clusters of countries

00:16:15.700 --> 00:16:19.250 align:middle line:84%
like Northern European
versus Southern European.

00:16:19.250 --> 00:16:22.900 align:middle line:84%
Those tend to be
separately highly

00:16:22.900 --> 00:16:25.750 align:middle line:90%
correlated with each other.

00:16:25.750 --> 00:16:29.080 align:middle line:84%
Another example of principal
components analysis

00:16:29.080 --> 00:16:36.220 align:middle line:84%
is looking at interest rates
in the fixed income market.

00:16:36.220 --> 00:16:42.330 align:middle line:84%
It turns out that for different
maturities of bonds ranging

00:16:42.330 --> 00:16:45.840 align:middle line:84%
from, say, one month,
three month, a year,

00:16:45.840 --> 00:16:52.110 align:middle line:84%
to five years, 10 years, 20
years, 30 years, the interest

00:16:52.110 --> 00:17:00.160 align:middle line:84%
rates can be measured in
terms of their yields.

00:17:00.160 --> 00:17:05.500 align:middle line:84%
And the yield curve
will vary day by day.

00:17:05.500 --> 00:17:08.910 align:middle line:84%
And it turns out that
the principal components

00:17:08.910 --> 00:17:17.075 align:middle line:84%
analysis of yields has a
very strong structure to it.

00:17:17.075 --> 00:17:20.016 align:middle line:90%


00:17:20.016 --> 00:17:24.359 align:middle line:84%
So I'll let him
introduce those when

00:17:24.359 --> 00:17:27.490 align:middle line:90%
he comes in a couple of weeks.

00:17:27.490 --> 00:17:31.350 align:middle line:84%
Well, with principal
component variables,

00:17:31.350 --> 00:17:36.680 align:middle line:84%
it's important to
understand that there

00:17:36.680 --> 00:17:41.480 align:middle line:84%
are alternative ways of deriving
or defining principal component

00:17:41.480 --> 00:17:42.600 align:middle line:90%
variables.

00:17:42.600 --> 00:17:47.030 align:middle line:84%
One is to define the first
principal component variable

00:17:47.030 --> 00:17:53.060 align:middle line:84%
to be the linear combination,
defined by weights w1

00:17:53.060 --> 00:18:00.140 align:middle line:84%
through wm, that maximize the
variance of that weighted sum

00:18:00.140 --> 00:18:05.010 align:middle line:84%
subject to the sum of squares
of the weights equaling 1.

00:18:05.010 --> 00:18:10.430 align:middle line:84%
So we're looking
at a unit vector w

00:18:10.430 --> 00:18:16.790 align:middle line:84%
and trying to find
the vector w that

00:18:16.790 --> 00:18:23.990 align:middle line:84%
maximizes the variance
of the linear combination

00:18:23.990 --> 00:18:25.320 align:middle line:90%
in that direction.

00:18:25.320 --> 00:18:33.350 align:middle line:84%
So when we get to the
transformation of the data--

00:18:33.350 --> 00:18:36.680 align:middle line:84%
or the random variable
by demeaning it,

00:18:36.680 --> 00:18:42.520 align:middle line:84%
then if we look
at w vectors that

00:18:42.520 --> 00:18:47.830 align:middle line:84%
are unit vectors in
this space, then we're

00:18:47.830 --> 00:18:52.420 align:middle line:84%
looking at which w
vector is pointing

00:18:52.420 --> 00:18:55.910 align:middle line:84%
in the direction of
maximum variability.

00:18:55.910 --> 00:18:58.810 align:middle line:84%
Then the second principal
component variable

00:18:58.810 --> 00:19:05.200 align:middle line:84%
is defined as the
next w vector, now

00:19:05.200 --> 00:19:10.090 align:middle line:84%
v, which maximizes the variance
subject to having length one

00:19:10.090 --> 00:19:12.730 align:middle line:84%
but being orthogonal
to the first.

00:19:12.730 --> 00:19:18.400 align:middle line:84%
So it's a rather nice
way of characterizing

00:19:18.400 --> 00:19:20.450 align:middle line:90%
principal component variables.

00:19:20.450 --> 00:19:24.800 align:middle line:84%
Now, with principal
components analysis,

00:19:24.800 --> 00:19:29.350 align:middle line:84%
one of the really
neat things is that--

00:19:29.350 --> 00:19:30.080 align:middle line:90%
let's see.

00:19:30.080 --> 00:19:34.140 align:middle line:84%
We often focus on the first few
principal component variables

00:19:34.140 --> 00:19:36.690 align:middle line:84%
because they explain
most of the variability.

00:19:36.690 --> 00:19:40.950 align:middle line:84%
But the last principal
component variable

00:19:40.950 --> 00:19:46.110 align:middle line:84%
defines the linear
combination of the components

00:19:46.110 --> 00:19:48.520 align:middle line:90%
that has the smallest variance.

00:19:48.520 --> 00:19:51.060 align:middle line:84%
And sometimes that
smallest variance

00:19:51.060 --> 00:19:54.030 align:middle line:84%
can be zero, in
which case, we're

00:19:54.030 --> 00:19:59.620 align:middle line:84%
identifying a linear
dependence in the returns.

00:19:59.620 --> 00:20:02.980 align:middle line:90%
So let's see.

00:20:02.980 --> 00:20:11.500 align:middle line:84%
My first example of this case
was before the euro existed.

00:20:11.500 --> 00:20:14.190 align:middle line:90%
So I'm really dating myself.

00:20:14.190 --> 00:20:19.380 align:middle line:84%
But the German deutschmark
and the French franc

00:20:19.380 --> 00:20:22.110 align:middle line:84%
were very, very
highly correlated

00:20:22.110 --> 00:20:24.400 align:middle line:90%
before the euro came out.

00:20:24.400 --> 00:20:28.200 align:middle line:84%
And in looking at
currency returns,

00:20:28.200 --> 00:20:30.000 align:middle line:84%
the principal
components analysis

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:33.960 align:middle line:84%
actually identified that
empirically in a nice way.

00:20:33.960 --> 00:20:37.900 align:middle line:90%


00:20:37.900 --> 00:20:38.810 align:middle line:90%
Let's see.

00:20:38.810 --> 00:20:45.290 align:middle line:84%
OK, mathematically with
principal components analysis,

00:20:45.290 --> 00:20:47.650 align:middle line:84%
we can talk about
a decomposition

00:20:47.650 --> 00:20:49.910 align:middle line:90%
of the total variance.

00:20:49.910 --> 00:20:55.150 align:middle line:84%
So if we have this
covariance matrix sigma,

00:20:55.150 --> 00:20:59.980 align:middle line:84%
we could talk about the total
variance is equal to the sum

00:20:59.980 --> 00:21:04.390 align:middle line:90%
1 to m of sigma jj.

00:21:04.390 --> 00:21:09.640 align:middle line:84%
So the j-th diagonal entry
of the covariance matrix

00:21:09.640 --> 00:21:12.580 align:middle line:84%
is the variance of
the j-th component.

00:21:12.580 --> 00:21:19.935 align:middle line:84%
And the sum of that
is the trace of sigma.

00:21:19.935 --> 00:21:22.690 align:middle line:90%


00:21:22.690 --> 00:21:27.220 align:middle line:84%
We can decompose
that total variance

00:21:27.220 --> 00:21:31.180 align:middle line:90%
as the sum of the eigenvalues.

00:21:31.180 --> 00:21:35.160 align:middle line:84%
And so principal
components analysis

00:21:35.160 --> 00:21:41.190 align:middle line:84%
provides a decomposition
of variability.

00:21:41.190 --> 00:21:44.850 align:middle line:84%
And we can see what
proportion of variability

00:21:44.850 --> 00:21:49.680 align:middle line:84%
is explained by the first or the
first few principal component

00:21:49.680 --> 00:21:52.560 align:middle line:90%
variables.

00:21:52.560 --> 00:21:56.700 align:middle line:84%
These lecture notes finish
with just reminding you

00:21:56.700 --> 00:22:00.690 align:middle line:90%
of some important statistic--

00:22:00.690 --> 00:22:06.180 align:middle line:84%
important probability
distributions in statistics.

00:22:06.180 --> 00:22:10.420 align:middle line:84%
The chi-squared distribution
arises again and again.

00:22:10.420 --> 00:22:15.030 align:middle line:84%
And the chi-squared distribution
with one degree of freedom

00:22:15.030 --> 00:22:19.410 align:middle line:84%
is equal in distribution
to the square

00:22:19.410 --> 00:22:22.290 align:middle line:84%
of a standard
normal distribution.

00:22:22.290 --> 00:22:30.200 align:middle line:84%
So this is a useful thing to
remember in different cases.

00:22:30.200 --> 00:22:32.210 align:middle line:90%
And let's see.

00:22:32.210 --> 00:22:37.910 align:middle line:84%
If we-- well, here's
its density function.

00:22:37.910 --> 00:22:41.970 align:middle line:84%
It turns out to be a special
case of the gamma distribution.

00:22:41.970 --> 00:22:46.410 align:middle line:84%
And here's this
moment-generating function.

00:22:46.410 --> 00:22:54.260 align:middle line:84%
It turns out that if we
take independent normal 0, 1

00:22:54.260 --> 00:22:57.060 align:middle line:84%
random variables, square
them and sum them,

00:22:57.060 --> 00:23:00.410 align:middle line:84%
then the sum of n independent
ones is a chi-squared with N

00:23:00.410 --> 00:23:02.570 align:middle line:90%
degrees of freedom.

00:23:02.570 --> 00:23:05.960 align:middle line:84%
And we can prove
these results using

00:23:05.960 --> 00:23:11.000 align:middle line:84%
moment-generating functions
and get densities for those.

00:23:11.000 --> 00:23:15.715 align:middle line:84%
Importantly, t
distributions often are--

00:23:15.715 --> 00:23:18.980 align:middle line:90%


00:23:18.980 --> 00:23:25.940 align:middle line:84%
let's see-- arise, especially in
the study of regression models

00:23:25.940 --> 00:23:29.920 align:middle line:84%
and the significance of
regression parameter estimates.

00:23:29.920 --> 00:23:35.470 align:middle line:84%
But basically, with
a t distribution,

00:23:35.470 --> 00:23:39.430 align:middle line:84%
if we take a standard
normal and then

00:23:39.430 --> 00:23:44.410 align:middle line:84%
divide it by the square root
of a chi-squared divided

00:23:44.410 --> 00:23:50.080 align:middle line:84%
by its degrees of freedom, where
the chi-squared variable U is

00:23:50.080 --> 00:23:53.620 align:middle line:84%
independent, then this will
give us a t distribution

00:23:53.620 --> 00:23:55.540 align:middle line:90%
with r degrees of freedom.

00:23:55.540 --> 00:24:00.280 align:middle line:90%
And so, let's see.

00:24:00.280 --> 00:24:05.830 align:middle line:84%
With t distributions,
t distributions

00:24:05.830 --> 00:24:10.540 align:middle line:84%
are examples of distributions
that are heavier tailed

00:24:10.540 --> 00:24:13.480 align:middle line:90%
than a normal distribution.

00:24:13.480 --> 00:24:20.230 align:middle line:84%
And so if you think of
taking a normal z variable

00:24:20.230 --> 00:24:25.030 align:middle line:84%
and dividing by this
chi-squared distribution divided

00:24:25.030 --> 00:24:29.050 align:middle line:84%
by this degree of
freedom and square root,

00:24:29.050 --> 00:24:33.870 align:middle line:84%
we're basically dividing a
random normal by something

00:24:33.870 --> 00:24:37.770 align:middle line:90%
that's random, but around 1.

00:24:37.770 --> 00:24:42.330 align:middle line:84%
And the randomness
of that factor

00:24:42.330 --> 00:24:45.700 align:middle line:84%
leads to greater
variability than the normal.

00:24:45.700 --> 00:24:49.210 align:middle line:84%
So it's obvious that
that could be the case.

00:24:49.210 --> 00:24:54.570 align:middle line:90%
And it's indeed true.

00:24:54.570 --> 00:24:58.110 align:middle line:90%
And let's see.

00:24:58.110 --> 00:25:01.710 align:middle line:84%
The F distribution
turns out to be

00:25:01.710 --> 00:25:07.690 align:middle line:84%
the ratio of two
chi-squared distributions,

00:25:07.690 --> 00:25:10.990 align:middle line:84%
each divided by their
respective degrees of freedom.

00:25:10.990 --> 00:25:16.620 align:middle line:84%
And in classical
statistics, we often

00:25:16.620 --> 00:25:22.050 align:middle line:84%
are interested in measuring
variability of data

00:25:22.050 --> 00:25:27.150 align:middle line:84%
and variability due to
different driving factors.

00:25:27.150 --> 00:25:31.190 align:middle line:84%
And so F distributions
arise commonly

00:25:31.190 --> 00:25:35.480 align:middle line:84%
when we have
estimates of variance

00:25:35.480 --> 00:25:39.330 align:middle line:84%
given by a chi-squared divided
by a degree of freedom.

00:25:39.330 --> 00:25:44.390 align:middle line:84%
And if we have two different
estimates of variability,

00:25:44.390 --> 00:25:51.450 align:middle line:84%
then if those distributions
have common variances,

00:25:51.450 --> 00:25:56.690 align:middle line:90%
then the F distribution applies.

00:25:56.690 --> 00:26:00.980 align:middle line:84%
Some of you may have heard
of analysis of variance

00:26:00.980 --> 00:26:02.790 align:middle line:90%
in statistics.

00:26:02.790 --> 00:26:05.120 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, the analysis
of variance methods

00:26:05.120 --> 00:26:10.340 align:middle line:84%
work with studying F
statistic distributions.

00:26:10.340 --> 00:26:11.817 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:26:11.817 --> 00:26:13.400 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Are
noncentral distributions

00:26:13.400 --> 00:26:16.260 align:middle line:84%
used a lot where there's
a noncentrality parameter?

00:26:16.260 --> 00:26:19.130 align:middle line:84%
Or are they mainly just
like the centered ones?

00:26:19.130 --> 00:26:22.310 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: Well,
the noncentrality--

00:26:22.310 --> 00:26:28.140 align:middle line:84%
or when you have null hypotheses
in analysis of variance

00:26:28.140 --> 00:26:32.340 align:middle line:84%
that are true, then
the F statistics

00:26:32.340 --> 00:26:37.260 align:middle line:84%
will be F's with
zero noncentrality.

00:26:37.260 --> 00:26:40.240 align:middle line:84%
And if the null
hypothesis is false,

00:26:40.240 --> 00:26:44.370 align:middle line:84%
then you'll have
a F distribution

00:26:44.370 --> 00:26:46.750 align:middle line:90%
with a noncentrality parameter.

00:26:46.750 --> 00:26:50.730 align:middle line:84%
So the noncentrality
parameter comes

00:26:50.730 --> 00:26:55.110 align:middle line:84%
into specifying the distribution
of the test statistic

00:26:55.110 --> 00:26:59.892 align:middle line:84%
when the null
hypothesis is false.

00:26:59.892 --> 00:27:01.600 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So it's not
used sometimes then

00:27:01.600 --> 00:27:03.490 align:middle line:90%
like when [INAUDIBLE] is false.

00:27:03.490 --> 00:27:05.110 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: Yes.

00:27:05.110 --> 00:27:09.450 align:middle line:84%
Actually, the next two
lectures after today

00:27:09.450 --> 00:27:14.380 align:middle line:84%
will be on regression analysis
and testing and regression.

00:27:14.380 --> 00:27:17.380 align:middle line:84%
And so we'll see those
come into play there.

00:27:17.380 --> 00:27:17.880 align:middle line:90%
Yep.

00:27:17.880 --> 00:27:20.700 align:middle line:90%


00:27:20.700 --> 00:27:21.990 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:27:21.990 --> 00:27:22.750 align:middle line:90%
What else?

00:27:22.750 --> 00:27:23.520 align:middle line:90%
Oh, OK.

00:27:23.520 --> 00:27:26.120 align:middle line:84%
Just the weak law of large
numbers and the central limit

00:27:26.120 --> 00:27:28.165 align:middle line:84%
theorem are the
remaining topics here.

00:27:28.165 --> 00:27:30.920 align:middle line:90%


00:27:30.920 --> 00:27:38.560 align:middle line:84%
If we have sample data
realizations of, say, N--

00:27:38.560 --> 00:27:41.330 align:middle line:90%


00:27:41.330 --> 00:27:45.170 align:middle line:84%
or N realizations of
a random variable--

00:27:45.170 --> 00:27:49.760 align:middle line:84%
and here, we say IID for
Independent and Identically

00:27:49.760 --> 00:27:52.610 align:middle line:90%
Distributed.

00:27:52.610 --> 00:27:57.290 align:middle line:84%
If those random variables
have mean mu and variance

00:27:57.290 --> 00:28:03.890 align:middle line:84%
sigma squared, then the
sample mean, X bar n,

00:28:03.890 --> 00:28:08.130 align:middle line:84%
will converge in
probability to that mean.

00:28:08.130 --> 00:28:12.690 align:middle line:84%
So the larger the
sample size in the mean,

00:28:12.690 --> 00:28:19.710 align:middle line:84%
the closer the sample mean will
be to the true mu parameter.

00:28:19.710 --> 00:28:23.770 align:middle line:84%
But what we-- what's important
from probability theory

00:28:23.770 --> 00:28:28.310 align:middle line:84%
is how do we characterize
convergence of random variables?

00:28:28.310 --> 00:28:33.190 align:middle line:84%
And so the convergence in
probability to a constant

00:28:33.190 --> 00:28:38.630 align:middle line:84%
means that the probability
that the deviation,

00:28:38.630 --> 00:28:41.860 align:middle line:84%
the absolute deviation
from mu exceeding epsilon,

00:28:41.860 --> 00:28:44.140 align:middle line:90%
that that goes to zero.

00:28:44.140 --> 00:28:49.570 align:middle line:84%
And this theorem--
or this result

00:28:49.570 --> 00:28:53.410 align:middle line:84%
can be proven with
Chebyshev's inequality.

00:28:53.410 --> 00:29:00.790 align:middle line:84%
And it's sort of a
neat example where

00:29:00.790 --> 00:29:07.810 align:middle line:84%
if we want to look at
the variance of X bar n,

00:29:07.810 --> 00:29:12.867 align:middle line:84%
which is equal to, what, the
expected value of X bar minus mu

00:29:12.867 --> 00:29:13.367 align:middle line:90%
squared.

00:29:13.367 --> 00:29:19.230 align:middle line:90%


00:29:19.230 --> 00:29:29.130 align:middle line:84%
Then if we were to draw
X bar n, and here's mu,

00:29:29.130 --> 00:29:36.660 align:middle line:84%
and then have the graph of
X bar n minus mu squared,

00:29:36.660 --> 00:29:44.370 align:middle line:84%
if we were to transform our
scale to X bar n minus mu,

00:29:44.370 --> 00:29:49.380 align:middle line:90%
then this mu value goes to zero.

00:29:49.380 --> 00:29:57.780 align:middle line:84%
And if we were to look at
the epsilon value for X bar n

00:29:57.780 --> 00:30:06.390 align:middle line:84%
minus mu, we can
consider a function

00:30:06.390 --> 00:30:11.670 align:middle line:84%
other than this
parabola, which is zero,

00:30:11.670 --> 00:30:17.280 align:middle line:84%
up to this epsilon
away from mu point,

00:30:17.280 --> 00:30:24.770 align:middle line:84%
and then is simply equal
to epsilon squared beyond,

00:30:24.770 --> 00:30:27.900 align:middle line:84%
and do the same
at minus epsilon.

00:30:27.900 --> 00:30:30.590 align:middle line:90%
So zero up to there.

00:30:30.590 --> 00:30:36.500 align:middle line:84%
And the variance, which is the
probability weighted average

00:30:36.500 --> 00:30:40.280 align:middle line:84%
of the parabola over
the distribution,

00:30:40.280 --> 00:30:47.360 align:middle line:84%
has to be greater than epsilon
squared times the probability

00:30:47.360 --> 00:30:50.300 align:middle line:90%
of exceeding--

00:30:50.300 --> 00:30:53.960 align:middle line:84%
of being further away
from you than epsilon.

00:30:53.960 --> 00:30:58.910 align:middle line:84%
So the fact that
this function is

00:30:58.910 --> 00:31:02.060 align:middle line:84%
greater than or equal to epsilon
squared times the probability

00:31:02.060 --> 00:31:07.760 align:middle line:84%
that X bar n minus mu
is greater than epsilon.

00:31:07.760 --> 00:31:18.190 align:middle line:84%
This gives us the result.
And we basically have that--

00:31:18.190 --> 00:31:28.600 align:middle line:84%
we're basically giving a
positive bound for this.

00:31:28.600 --> 00:31:30.850 align:middle line:84%
Or we have an upper
bound that decreases

00:31:30.850 --> 00:31:33.110 align:middle line:90%
with n for this probability.

00:31:33.110 --> 00:31:38.680 align:middle line:84%
So that gives us
our nice result.

00:31:38.680 --> 00:31:39.250 align:middle line:90%
Let's see.

00:31:39.250 --> 00:31:43.300 align:middle line:84%
If you really like
probability theory,

00:31:43.300 --> 00:31:47.350 align:middle line:84%
one can actually extend
this result to the case

00:31:47.350 --> 00:31:51.800 align:middle line:84%
where we don't have
a variance existing.

00:31:51.800 --> 00:31:54.130 align:middle line:90%
We just have a mean existing.

00:31:54.130 --> 00:32:00.100 align:middle line:84%
And that's a rather
strong result that's neat.

00:32:00.100 --> 00:32:03.820 align:middle line:84%
At least it's neat in terms
of how the mathematics can

00:32:03.820 --> 00:32:08.270 align:middle line:90%
handle more complex cases.

00:32:08.270 --> 00:32:12.740 align:middle line:84%
Now, finally, we have the
central limit theorem,

00:32:12.740 --> 00:32:18.860 align:middle line:84%
which tells us what the
asymptotic distribution

00:32:18.860 --> 00:32:26.990 align:middle line:84%
is of a sum of independent
and identically distributed

00:32:26.990 --> 00:32:29.030 align:middle line:90%
random variables is.

00:32:29.030 --> 00:32:36.680 align:middle line:84%
And so if we have
variables X1 to Xn

00:32:36.680 --> 00:32:40.580 align:middle line:84%
that are mean 0
variance sigma squared,

00:32:40.580 --> 00:32:47.360 align:middle line:84%
it turns out that the
sum of the excise divided

00:32:47.360 --> 00:32:51.560 align:middle line:84%
by the square root
of the sample size

00:32:51.560 --> 00:32:55.490 align:middle line:84%
converges to a normal
distribution with mean zero

00:32:55.490 --> 00:32:57.230 align:middle line:90%
and variance sigma squared.

00:32:57.230 --> 00:33:06.050 align:middle line:84%
So we have, what, Zn equaling
1 over root n sum 1 to n

00:33:06.050 --> 00:33:10.730 align:middle line:84%
of the Xi's converges to
a normal with mean zero

00:33:10.730 --> 00:33:12.600 align:middle line:90%
and variance sigma squared.

00:33:12.600 --> 00:33:20.950 align:middle line:84%
And if we were to
think of X bar, which

00:33:20.950 --> 00:33:25.660 align:middle line:84%
is equal to 1 over
root n times Zn,

00:33:25.660 --> 00:33:28.690 align:middle line:84%
this basically is
converging, in some sense,

00:33:28.690 --> 00:33:34.940 align:middle line:84%
to a normal with mean zero and
variance sigma squared over n.

00:33:34.940 --> 00:33:38.980 align:middle line:84%
Now, I put quotes
around this arrow

00:33:38.980 --> 00:33:43.300 align:middle line:84%
here because it's not
really legitimate to talk

00:33:43.300 --> 00:33:48.920 align:middle line:84%
about a limiting distribution
that depends on n.

00:33:48.920 --> 00:33:56.770 align:middle line:84%
But this is the way we
approximate the distributions

00:33:56.770 --> 00:33:58.070 align:middle line:90%
of sample means.

00:33:58.070 --> 00:34:02.680 align:middle line:84%
Basically, they converge to
the mean of the distribution,

00:34:02.680 --> 00:34:03.980 align:middle line:90%
in this case, zero.

00:34:03.980 --> 00:34:06.800 align:middle line:84%
And the variance is
sigma squared over n.

00:34:06.800 --> 00:34:11.290 align:middle line:84%
Now, what's neat in
this proof is just

00:34:11.290 --> 00:34:17.250 align:middle line:84%
to highlight the use of
moment-generating functions.

00:34:17.250 --> 00:34:19.110 align:middle line:90%
And so if we--

00:34:19.110 --> 00:34:21.929 align:middle line:84%
say, let's calculate the
moment-generating function

00:34:21.929 --> 00:34:28.050 align:middle line:84%
of Zn, we're going to show that
the moment-generating function

00:34:28.050 --> 00:34:32.260 align:middle line:84%
of Zn converges to this
moment-generating function,

00:34:32.260 --> 00:34:35.850 align:middle line:84%
which in fact is the
normal with mean zero

00:34:35.850 --> 00:34:37.389 align:middle line:90%
and variance sigma squared.

00:34:37.389 --> 00:34:41.010 align:middle line:84%
So the theorem that says
moment-generating functions

00:34:41.010 --> 00:34:46.739 align:middle line:84%
are of unique, and if the limit
of moment-generating functions

00:34:46.739 --> 00:34:51.900 align:middle line:84%
exists and equals
some function, we

00:34:51.900 --> 00:34:54.929 align:middle line:84%
can identify the
distribution by that limiting

00:34:54.929 --> 00:34:56.710 align:middle line:90%
moment-generating function.

00:34:56.710 --> 00:35:00.990 align:middle line:84%
Well, if we calculate just
the moment-generating function

00:35:00.990 --> 00:35:07.980 align:middle line:84%
of Zn, if we just plug-in
Zn within the expectation,

00:35:07.980 --> 00:35:14.270 align:middle line:84%
then noting that the X's are
independent of each other,

00:35:14.270 --> 00:35:18.890 align:middle line:84%
we basically are taking the
expectation of a product

00:35:18.890 --> 00:35:21.390 align:middle line:90%
of independent terms.

00:35:21.390 --> 00:35:24.560 align:middle line:84%
And so that expectation
of a product

00:35:24.560 --> 00:35:27.350 align:middle line:84%
is the product of
the expectations.

00:35:27.350 --> 00:35:32.330 align:middle line:84%
So we have the product
of the expected value

00:35:32.330 --> 00:35:35.210 align:middle line:90%
of Xi times t over root n.

00:35:35.210 --> 00:35:38.900 align:middle line:84%
And this expectation
here is actually

00:35:38.900 --> 00:35:48.560 align:middle line:84%
the moment-generating function
of X evaluated at t over root n.

00:35:48.560 --> 00:35:52.130 align:middle line:84%
So it's the n-th power
of that when we take

00:35:52.130 --> 00:35:54.620 align:middle line:90%
the product of all n of those.

00:35:54.620 --> 00:35:58.790 align:middle line:84%
Now, this is where
it gets interesting.

00:35:58.790 --> 00:36:02.540 align:middle line:84%
If we look at our
moment-generating function of t

00:36:02.540 --> 00:36:07.790 align:middle line:84%
over root n, well, we don't
really know much about

00:36:07.790 --> 00:36:09.720 align:middle line:90%
the distribution of the X's.

00:36:09.720 --> 00:36:11.180 align:middle line:90%
We know it has a mean.

00:36:11.180 --> 00:36:13.270 align:middle line:90%
We know it has a variance.

00:36:13.270 --> 00:36:14.660 align:middle line:90%
The mean is zero.

00:36:14.660 --> 00:36:16.870 align:middle line:90%
The variance is sigma squared.

00:36:16.870 --> 00:36:20.590 align:middle line:84%
If we expand the
moment-generating function

00:36:20.590 --> 00:36:25.870 align:middle line:84%
into a Taylor
series, then we get

00:36:25.870 --> 00:36:31.060 align:middle line:84%
1 plus the expected value
of X times t over root n

00:36:31.060 --> 00:36:36.130 align:middle line:84%
plus the expected value
of X squared divided

00:36:36.130 --> 00:36:39.220 align:middle line:84%
by 2 times t over
root n squared plus,

00:36:39.220 --> 00:36:46.730 align:middle line:84%
an order term that is given by
the third power of the argument.

00:36:46.730 --> 00:36:53.620 align:middle line:84%
And so as n gets large,
these higher order terms

00:36:53.620 --> 00:36:56.180 align:middle line:90%
are of smaller order.

00:36:56.180 --> 00:37:01.450 align:middle line:84%
And so we finally have
that this in the limit

00:37:01.450 --> 00:37:06.610 align:middle line:84%
is 1 plus this multiple
over n plus a small o of t

00:37:06.610 --> 00:37:08.260 align:middle line:90%
squared over n.

00:37:08.260 --> 00:37:15.330 align:middle line:84%
And if we take this
expression to the n-th power,

00:37:15.330 --> 00:37:22.050 align:middle line:84%
then it converges to the
exponent of the argument

00:37:22.050 --> 00:37:25.980 align:middle line:90%
sigma squared t squared over 2.

00:37:25.980 --> 00:37:34.920 align:middle line:84%
So let's see if
we have 1 plus r--

00:37:34.920 --> 00:37:43.830 align:middle line:84%
let's -- 1 plus r over
n to the n-th power,

00:37:43.830 --> 00:37:46.445 align:middle line:90%
this converges to e to the r.

00:37:46.445 --> 00:37:49.080 align:middle line:90%


00:37:49.080 --> 00:37:55.470 align:middle line:84%
And Vasiliy used this
argument in the first lecture

00:37:55.470 --> 00:37:59.130 align:middle line:84%
to talk about
continuous compounding

00:37:59.130 --> 00:38:04.710 align:middle line:84%
and what the limiting value
is of continuous compounding

00:38:04.710 --> 00:38:06.190 align:middle line:90%
at some interest rate.

00:38:06.190 --> 00:38:13.010 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, that same exponential
result is true here.

00:38:13.010 --> 00:38:17.620 align:middle line:84%
So this actually proves the
central limit theorem using

00:38:17.620 --> 00:38:20.570 align:middle line:90%
the identity theorem for MGFs.

00:38:20.570 --> 00:38:21.130 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:38:21.130 --> 00:38:24.220 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Is CLT actually
used in practice?

00:38:24.220 --> 00:38:26.980 align:middle line:84%
Because obviously,
like in finance nothing

00:38:26.980 --> 00:38:29.513 align:middle line:84%
is going to be IID,
or at least they're

00:38:29.513 --> 00:38:30.680 align:middle line:90%
not going to be independent.

00:38:30.680 --> 00:38:34.690 align:middle line:84%
So is it actually still
useful in a practical setting?

00:38:34.690 --> 00:38:38.300 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: It can be very
useful in a practical setting.

00:38:38.300 --> 00:38:45.250 align:middle line:84%
And right now, it's specified in
terms of identically distributed

00:38:45.250 --> 00:38:46.870 align:middle line:90%
X's.

00:38:46.870 --> 00:38:52.150 align:middle line:84%
It actually generalizes
to X's that are

00:38:52.150 --> 00:38:59.210 align:middle line:84%
independent but with different
distributions as well.

00:38:59.210 --> 00:39:09.630 align:middle line:84%
So also what's I guess really
important is that if a variable

00:39:09.630 --> 00:39:14.940 align:middle line:84%
we're working with
corresponds to the sum

00:39:14.940 --> 00:39:21.070 align:middle line:84%
of independent or
uncorrelated terms,

00:39:21.070 --> 00:39:26.350 align:middle line:84%
the sum can approach a
normal distribution well.

00:39:26.350 --> 00:39:36.450 align:middle line:84%
And if one looks at, I
guess, linear projections

00:39:36.450 --> 00:39:44.070 align:middle line:84%
of multidimensional
vectors, X's,

00:39:44.070 --> 00:39:51.630 align:middle line:84%
if you had a random direction
in the n dimensional space,

00:39:51.630 --> 00:39:55.320 align:middle line:84%
the projection onto
that random direction

00:39:55.320 --> 00:40:02.790 align:middle line:84%
will correspond to sums of
close to identically distributed

00:40:02.790 --> 00:40:08.390 align:middle line:84%
random variables, but will
very likely have a Gaussian

00:40:08.390 --> 00:40:11.900 align:middle line:90%
distribution as well.

00:40:11.900 --> 00:40:17.540 align:middle line:84%
This will come up when we talk
about regression modeling.

00:40:17.540 --> 00:40:23.090 align:middle line:84%
But when we look at the error
vector from a regression,

00:40:23.090 --> 00:40:31.370 align:middle line:84%
the feasibility of the error
vector being close to Gaussian

00:40:31.370 --> 00:40:36.050 align:middle line:84%
is plausible and
reasonable given the way

00:40:36.050 --> 00:40:38.540 align:middle line:90%
it's constructed sometimes.

00:40:38.540 --> 00:40:39.630 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:40:39.630 --> 00:40:47.270 align:middle line:84%
Well, let's move on then
to this note on probability

00:40:47.270 --> 00:40:49.200 align:middle line:90%
theory for asset pricing.

00:40:49.200 --> 00:40:52.670 align:middle line:84%
What's really neat
about this note

00:40:52.670 --> 00:40:58.370 align:middle line:84%
is that we're going to introduce
you just with basic probability

00:40:58.370 --> 00:41:02.030 align:middle line:84%
theory to one of the
more important models

00:41:02.030 --> 00:41:06.370 align:middle line:84%
in quantitative
finance, or at least

00:41:06.370 --> 00:41:09.160 align:middle line:84%
in early quantitative finance,
which is the capital asset

00:41:09.160 --> 00:41:10.900 align:middle line:90%
pricing model.

00:41:10.900 --> 00:41:21.580 align:middle line:84%
And so let's consider a
simple context where we have

00:41:21.580 --> 00:41:25.940 align:middle line:90%
one risky asset, like a stock.

00:41:25.940 --> 00:41:34.210 align:middle line:84%
And we have a one period
model where we have--

00:41:34.210 --> 00:41:42.550 align:middle line:84%
the beginning of the period is
t0, and the end of the period

00:41:42.550 --> 00:41:44.520 align:middle line:90%
is t1, I guess.

00:41:44.520 --> 00:41:53.050 align:middle line:90%


00:41:53.050 --> 00:41:58.330 align:middle line:84%
And there's this
risky asset, a--

00:41:58.330 --> 00:42:03.020 align:middle line:90%
so there's a risky asset, a.

00:42:03.020 --> 00:42:06.085 align:middle line:90%


00:42:06.085 --> 00:42:13.530 align:middle line:84%
And we'll consider
a market agent

00:42:13.530 --> 00:42:26.970 align:middle line:84%
having Qa shares of
the asset, and just

00:42:26.970 --> 00:42:29.103 align:middle line:90%
Q units of a risk-free asset.

00:42:29.103 --> 00:42:33.630 align:middle line:90%


00:42:33.630 --> 00:42:39.750 align:middle line:84%
Now, the risk-free asset is
like putting money in the bank

00:42:39.750 --> 00:42:45.090 align:middle line:84%
and being confident that you'll
get it back with interest.

00:42:45.090 --> 00:42:55.680 align:middle line:84%
And we'll assume that the
risk-free asset costs $1--

00:42:55.680 --> 00:42:59.730 align:middle line:90%
so $1 per unit.

00:42:59.730 --> 00:43:07.395 align:middle line:84%
And the risky asset will
be of price Pa per share.

00:43:07.395 --> 00:43:11.890 align:middle line:90%


00:43:11.890 --> 00:43:19.070 align:middle line:84%
So this market agent
has a wealth at time 0.

00:43:19.070 --> 00:43:28.865 align:middle line:84%
So W0 is going to be equal
to qa pa plus q times $1.

00:43:28.865 --> 00:43:31.450 align:middle line:90%


00:43:31.450 --> 00:43:33.670 align:middle line:84%
And X-- OK, so
maybe we'll put an f

00:43:33.670 --> 00:43:39.070 align:middle line:84%
here just to indicate that
it's the risk-free asset.

00:43:39.070 --> 00:43:40.557 align:middle line:90%
Now, at time T1--

00:43:40.557 --> 00:43:45.040 align:middle line:90%


00:43:45.040 --> 00:43:53.140 align:middle line:84%
so this is the t0
prices are given here.

00:43:53.140 --> 00:44:00.165 align:middle line:84%
At T1, the risk-free asset is
going to be worth 1 plus rf.

00:44:00.165 --> 00:44:07.050 align:middle line:90%


00:44:07.050 --> 00:44:12.960 align:middle line:84%
And the price of the
risky asset, we'll

00:44:12.960 --> 00:44:18.720 align:middle line:84%
assume that it's a random
variable given by f tilde.

00:44:18.720 --> 00:44:28.230 align:middle line:84%
And for simplicity, we'll assume
that it has some mean, mu,

00:44:28.230 --> 00:44:29.800 align:middle line:90%
and variance sigma squared.

00:44:29.800 --> 00:44:34.050 align:middle line:84%
Now, right away this is
maybe a bad assumption

00:44:34.050 --> 00:44:37.530 align:middle line:84%
to make because Gaussian
or normal distributions

00:44:37.530 --> 00:44:41.020 align:middle line:84%
can be arbitrarily
large negative.

00:44:41.020 --> 00:44:46.440 align:middle line:84%
But we'll assume that the
likelihood of impossible values

00:44:46.440 --> 00:44:51.910 align:middle line:84%
is very close to 0, so that
this is a useful model.

00:44:51.910 --> 00:45:01.730 align:middle line:84%
So this is the time T1
state of the market, which

00:45:01.730 --> 00:45:09.680 align:middle line:84%
has certainty for the
risk-free investment

00:45:09.680 --> 00:45:15.350 align:middle line:84%
but uncertain outcome
for the risky asset.

00:45:15.350 --> 00:45:24.170 align:middle line:84%
Well, one could
consider the agent

00:45:24.170 --> 00:45:31.250 align:middle line:84%
being able to buy x
units of the risky asset

00:45:31.250 --> 00:45:36.950 align:middle line:84%
at the initial time
point, t0, and then

00:45:36.950 --> 00:45:43.640 align:middle line:84%
have end of period wealth that
reflects that trade of buying

00:45:43.640 --> 00:45:46.080 align:middle line:90%
X units at t0.

00:45:46.080 --> 00:45:55.940 align:middle line:84%
So at the end of the
period t1, time, there

00:45:55.940 --> 00:46:01.720 align:middle line:84%
will be Qa plus X shares
with value f tilde.

00:46:01.720 --> 00:46:10.420 align:middle line:84%
And the x shares will
have been bought by paying

00:46:10.420 --> 00:46:14.530 align:middle line:90%
Pa, the t0 price at time 0.

00:46:14.530 --> 00:46:17.200 align:middle line:84%
And so there will
be money invested

00:46:17.200 --> 00:46:21.610 align:middle line:84%
in the risk-free asset
earning the interest rate,

00:46:21.610 --> 00:46:25.240 align:middle line:84%
or the absolute
return of 1 plus rf.

00:46:25.240 --> 00:46:30.340 align:middle line:84%
Now, what we want to
do is consider what

00:46:30.340 --> 00:46:33.490 align:middle line:90%
is the optimal choice of X--

00:46:33.490 --> 00:46:37.130 align:middle line:84%
the number of shares
to buy for this agent?

00:46:37.130 --> 00:46:42.290 align:middle line:84%
And in order to
make that choice,

00:46:42.290 --> 00:46:51.910 align:middle line:84%
one can apply the decision
theory under uncertainty

00:46:51.910 --> 00:46:58.050 align:middle line:84%
and associate a utility of
the end period wealth given

00:46:58.050 --> 00:47:02.100 align:middle line:90%
by some utility function, u.

00:47:02.100 --> 00:47:06.930 align:middle line:84%
And we can choose the
X value to maximize

00:47:06.930 --> 00:47:10.300 align:middle line:90%
the expected utility of wealth.

00:47:10.300 --> 00:47:21.240 align:middle line:84%
So importantly, If we have
u of w as a function of w,

00:47:21.240 --> 00:47:28.205 align:middle line:84%
and there's basically
a w0 value here--

00:47:28.205 --> 00:47:31.290 align:middle line:90%


00:47:31.290 --> 00:47:37.230 align:middle line:84%
utility of w0-- there are
some standard assumptions

00:47:37.230 --> 00:47:44.010 align:middle line:84%
one makes as to what the
utility function is like.

00:47:44.010 --> 00:47:48.060 align:middle line:84%
And we might assume that
the first derivative

00:47:48.060 --> 00:47:49.903 align:middle line:90%
and second derivative exist.

00:47:49.903 --> 00:47:52.920 align:middle line:90%


00:47:52.920 --> 00:47:57.540 align:middle line:84%
So we'll assume
that these exist.

00:47:57.540 --> 00:48:03.170 align:middle line:84%
And so-- well, the
first derivative

00:48:03.170 --> 00:48:06.380 align:middle line:90%
is likely to be positive.

00:48:06.380 --> 00:48:09.740 align:middle line:84%
More wealth is
preferred to less.

00:48:09.740 --> 00:48:16.140 align:middle line:84%
So we have a better direction
being up and to the right.

00:48:16.140 --> 00:48:23.420 align:middle line:84%
And the second derivative, this
actually could be less than 0

00:48:23.420 --> 00:48:29.160 align:middle line:84%
if there's a decreasing
marginal utility to more wealth.

00:48:29.160 --> 00:48:34.100 align:middle line:84%
So this function
might go and look

00:48:34.100 --> 00:48:39.290 align:middle line:84%
like this with a U prime greater
than 0 and U double prime less

00:48:39.290 --> 00:48:39.925 align:middle line:90%
than 0.

00:48:39.925 --> 00:48:43.280 align:middle line:90%


00:48:43.280 --> 00:48:47.400 align:middle line:84%
So these kinds of assumptions
are reasonable to assume.

00:48:47.400 --> 00:48:53.550 align:middle line:84%
And the theory of
rational decision-making

00:48:53.550 --> 00:48:58.350 align:middle line:84%
under uncertainty, if this is
indeed your utility function,

00:48:58.350 --> 00:49:04.660 align:middle line:84%
is to find the action
X that maximizes this.

00:49:04.660 --> 00:49:10.410 align:middle line:84%
And if we are lucky, we
might be able to solve

00:49:10.410 --> 00:49:13.890 align:middle line:84%
for X by satisfying
the first order

00:49:13.890 --> 00:49:21.030 align:middle line:84%
condition, the derivative of
the expected utility equaling 0

00:49:21.030 --> 00:49:24.060 align:middle line:90%
at the maximum value.

00:49:24.060 --> 00:49:26.910 align:middle line:90%
And-- yeah?

00:49:26.910 --> 00:49:31.020 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Why would you use
anything other than the identity

00:49:31.020 --> 00:49:31.900 align:middle line:90%
function for U?

00:49:31.900 --> 00:49:33.270 align:middle line:84%
Because surely you
just want to maximize

00:49:33.270 --> 00:49:34.353 align:middle line:90%
your end of period wealth?

00:49:34.353 --> 00:49:38.525 align:middle line:90%


00:49:38.525 --> 00:49:39.400 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: Let's see.

00:49:39.400 --> 00:49:47.190 align:middle line:84%
If you use the
identity, then there's--

00:49:47.190 --> 00:49:51.840 align:middle line:84%
well, it will turn out
there's no penalty for a risk

00:49:51.840 --> 00:49:54.150 align:middle line:90%
that you're undertaking.

00:49:54.150 --> 00:50:00.880 align:middle line:84%
And so with this decreasing
marginal utility of wealth,

00:50:00.880 --> 00:50:05.340 align:middle line:84%
you end up having a
penalty for taking risks.

00:50:05.340 --> 00:50:19.440 align:middle line:90%
And so the-- yeah.

00:50:19.440 --> 00:50:24.480 align:middle line:84%
So I'll leave it there, but
it's a very good question.

00:50:24.480 --> 00:50:27.540 align:middle line:90%
And let's see.

00:50:27.540 --> 00:50:32.430 align:middle line:84%
There's actually
some-- well, there's

00:50:32.430 --> 00:50:36.750 align:middle line:84%
some really interesting
discussions and about

00:50:36.750 --> 00:50:43.090 align:middle line:84%
how to optimally
invest, as this case.

00:50:43.090 --> 00:50:47.700 align:middle line:84%
And there was a controversy
between Claude Shannon

00:50:47.700 --> 00:50:58.360 align:middle line:84%
and I think it was Milton
Friedman about maximizing

00:50:58.360 --> 00:51:04.030 align:middle line:84%
the exponential--
or the expected log

00:51:04.030 --> 00:51:09.920 align:middle line:84%
return of investments just
period and that being optimal.

00:51:09.920 --> 00:51:15.140 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, we'll return to
that discussion later as well.

00:51:15.140 --> 00:51:17.740 align:middle line:84%
But for now, let's assume that
this is a reasonable thing

00:51:17.740 --> 00:51:18.710 align:middle line:90%
to do.

00:51:18.710 --> 00:51:23.750 align:middle line:84%
And in order to
solve this problem,

00:51:23.750 --> 00:51:26.290 align:middle line:84%
we're actually going
to use a lemma called

00:51:26.290 --> 00:51:33.550 align:middle line:84%
Stein's Lemma, which is a
curious mathematical result

00:51:33.550 --> 00:51:41.050 align:middle line:84%
dealing with normally
distributed y variable and y

00:51:41.050 --> 00:51:41.660 align:middle line:90%
star.

00:51:41.660 --> 00:51:47.150 align:middle line:84%
They're jointly distributed
with each other and Gaussian.

00:51:47.150 --> 00:51:55.680 align:middle line:84%
If we look at the expected value
of g of Y times Y minus mu,

00:51:55.680 --> 00:52:01.470 align:middle line:84%
and the expected value of g
prime of Y, if both of those

00:52:01.470 --> 00:52:04.740 align:middle line:84%
exist, then we get
these simple formulas

00:52:04.740 --> 00:52:10.380 align:middle line:84%
for essentially the covariance
between g of Y and Y,

00:52:10.380 --> 00:52:13.720 align:middle line:84%
and the covariance of
g and Y and Y star.

00:52:13.720 --> 00:52:20.700 align:middle line:84%
So this is a mathematical
theorem that is abstract

00:52:20.700 --> 00:52:27.630 align:middle line:84%
as it's stated, but it ends up
being applicable in our problem.

00:52:27.630 --> 00:52:33.420 align:middle line:84%
So if we define our wealth at
the end of the period at time t1

00:52:33.420 --> 00:52:39.600 align:middle line:84%
to be given by this
w tilde vector, then

00:52:39.600 --> 00:52:47.300 align:middle line:84%
if the outcome of the
asset price, f tilde,

00:52:47.300 --> 00:52:56.390 align:middle line:84%
if this is Gaussian, then
this wealth at time t1

00:52:56.390 --> 00:52:58.580 align:middle line:84%
will also be Gaussian
because we're just

00:52:58.580 --> 00:53:03.020 align:middle line:84%
taking a multiple of
that plus a constant.

00:53:03.020 --> 00:53:08.060 align:middle line:90%
So that note is simple.

00:53:08.060 --> 00:53:14.180 align:middle line:84%
Then if we look at
the expected utility

00:53:14.180 --> 00:53:20.690 align:middle line:84%
and try to take the derivative
of that with respect to X,

00:53:20.690 --> 00:53:27.080 align:middle line:84%
then under certain conditions
we can take the expectation

00:53:27.080 --> 00:53:33.050 align:middle line:84%
of the derivative with
respect to X of the integrand

00:53:33.050 --> 00:53:35.310 align:middle line:90%
with the expectation.

00:53:35.310 --> 00:53:41.420 align:middle line:84%
Now, under what conditions is
it feasible to differentiate

00:53:41.420 --> 00:53:45.380 align:middle line:84%
underneath the integral
sign when you're

00:53:45.380 --> 00:53:48.073 align:middle line:90%
doing computations in calculus?

00:53:48.073 --> 00:54:01.990 align:middle line:90%


00:54:01.990 --> 00:54:04.450 align:middle line:84%
I mean, basically
what we want to do

00:54:04.450 --> 00:54:16.540 align:middle line:84%
is take the expected value
of U of w tilde plus delta

00:54:16.540 --> 00:54:24.790 align:middle line:84%
and subtract the expectation
of U of w tilde and divide

00:54:24.790 --> 00:54:32.620 align:middle line:84%
by delta, and take the
limit as this goes to 0.

00:54:32.620 --> 00:54:36.076 align:middle line:84%
So that's the formula for
computing the derivative.

00:54:36.076 --> 00:54:39.520 align:middle line:90%


00:54:39.520 --> 00:54:46.490 align:middle line:84%
It turns out we can do that
if the first derivative of U

00:54:46.490 --> 00:54:51.050 align:middle line:84%
is bounded or has
bounded expectation.

00:54:51.050 --> 00:54:55.730 align:middle line:84%
And so if that's
possible to do, then we

00:54:55.730 --> 00:54:58.740 align:middle line:84%
can take the derivative
underneath the integral sign.

00:54:58.740 --> 00:55:01.430 align:middle line:84%
And we have basically
the derivative

00:55:01.430 --> 00:55:09.080 align:middle line:84%
of U multiplied by the chain
rule, the derivative of w tilde

00:55:09.080 --> 00:55:14.180 align:middle line:84%
with respect to X. So if
we take the derivative

00:55:14.180 --> 00:55:19.790 align:middle line:84%
of w tilde with respect to
X, we get f tilde minus Pa

00:55:19.790 --> 00:55:21.450 align:middle line:90%
times 1 plus rf.

00:55:21.450 --> 00:55:25.370 align:middle line:84%
So this is just the chain
rule of differentiation there.

00:55:25.370 --> 00:55:37.040 align:middle line:84%
Now with this expression
in black, we can use the--

00:55:37.040 --> 00:55:38.280 align:middle line:90%
well, let's see.

00:55:38.280 --> 00:55:46.000 align:middle line:84%
We can express this as the
expected product of two

00:55:46.000 --> 00:55:50.680 align:middle line:84%
random variables is equal to
the covariance plus the product

00:55:50.680 --> 00:55:52.540 align:middle line:90%
of the expectations.

00:55:52.540 --> 00:55:59.660 align:middle line:84%
So here, we have U prime and
f tilde minus Pa1 plus rf.

00:55:59.660 --> 00:56:03.400 align:middle line:84%
The covariance between
those two plus the product

00:56:03.400 --> 00:56:08.830 align:middle line:84%
of their expectations is equal
to that expected product.

00:56:08.830 --> 00:56:16.360 align:middle line:84%
Then, by Stein's Lemma,
one actually can simplify

00:56:16.360 --> 00:56:22.930 align:middle line:84%
or re-express this covariance as
the expected second derivative

00:56:22.930 --> 00:56:31.210 align:middle line:84%
of U times the
covariance of w and f.

00:56:31.210 --> 00:56:37.900 align:middle line:84%
And so we can then
substitute this expression

00:56:37.900 --> 00:56:43.050 align:middle line:84%
into this last
expression here and get 0

00:56:43.050 --> 00:56:47.640 align:middle line:84%
equals that covariance
plus the product

00:56:47.640 --> 00:56:52.200 align:middle line:90%
of the first expectations there.

00:56:52.200 --> 00:56:59.520 align:middle line:84%
And so we're substituting in
this expected second derivative

00:56:59.520 --> 00:57:04.270 align:middle line:84%
of U times the covariance
in that formula.

00:57:04.270 --> 00:57:12.510 align:middle line:84%
Now, with this
expression here, we

00:57:12.510 --> 00:57:15.870 align:middle line:90%
have this covariance of w and f.

00:57:15.870 --> 00:57:19.290 align:middle line:84%
Well, the covariance
between w and f

00:57:19.290 --> 00:57:28.210 align:middle line:84%
is simply the covariance
of f with a multiple of f.

00:57:28.210 --> 00:57:33.080 align:middle line:84%
So it equals that multiple
times the variance of f.

00:57:33.080 --> 00:57:35.610 align:middle line:90%


00:57:35.610 --> 00:57:40.880 align:middle line:84%
And so we plug that in
to this covariance term.

00:57:40.880 --> 00:57:46.500 align:middle line:84%
And what's curious,
and is the punch line,

00:57:46.500 --> 00:57:50.720 align:middle line:84%
is that this final
equation gives us

00:57:50.720 --> 00:58:06.860 align:middle line:84%
a formula for how
we solve for X.

00:58:06.860 --> 00:58:14.060 align:middle line:84%
And so the value of X
that we should choose

00:58:14.060 --> 00:58:20.060 align:middle line:84%
will basically
satisfy this equation.

00:58:20.060 --> 00:58:29.480 align:middle line:84%
And so our X value will depend
upon what the price of the asset

00:58:29.480 --> 00:58:30.390 align:middle line:90%
is.

00:58:30.390 --> 00:58:38.450 align:middle line:84%
And it actually is equal
to a discounted value

00:58:38.450 --> 00:58:47.920 align:middle line:84%
of the expected price
plus this other term here.

00:58:47.920 --> 00:58:50.480 align:middle line:90%
And this other term here--

00:58:50.480 --> 00:58:53.710 align:middle line:84%
the second derivative
is negative.

00:58:53.710 --> 00:58:55.580 align:middle line:90%
First derivative is positive.

00:58:55.580 --> 00:58:59.050 align:middle line:84%
So this is actually a
negative factor here.

00:58:59.050 --> 00:59:01.813 align:middle line:90%
And so we end up getting that--

00:59:01.813 --> 00:59:05.230 align:middle line:90%


00:59:05.230 --> 00:59:13.960 align:middle line:84%
we have a formula that
depends upon that ratio

00:59:13.960 --> 00:59:18.190 align:middle line:84%
of second derivative expectation
to first derivative expectation

00:59:18.190 --> 00:59:20.900 align:middle line:90%
of the utility function.

00:59:20.900 --> 00:59:25.330 align:middle line:84%
Now, in economics
classes, maybe some of you

00:59:25.330 --> 00:59:30.820 align:middle line:84%
have come across the constant
absolute risk aversion utility

00:59:30.820 --> 00:59:31.900 align:middle line:90%
function.

00:59:31.900 --> 00:59:35.620 align:middle line:84%
Has anybody come across
that in an economics class?

00:59:35.620 --> 00:59:36.120 align:middle line:90%
Maybe.

00:59:36.120 --> 00:59:38.370 align:middle line:90%
Maybe not.

00:59:38.370 --> 00:59:48.060 align:middle line:84%
But that function, which
is a constant minus

00:59:48.060 --> 01:00:01.500 align:middle line:84%
e to the minus Aw, this is the
constant absolute risk aversion

01:00:01.500 --> 01:00:03.220 align:middle line:90%
utility function.

01:00:03.220 --> 01:00:16.830 align:middle line:84%
And so it basically
has the right shape

01:00:16.830 --> 01:00:20.760 align:middle line:84%
in terms of marginal
utilities decreasing

01:00:20.760 --> 01:00:25.570 align:middle line:90%
and utilities always increasing.

01:00:25.570 --> 01:00:30.360 align:middle line:84%
And so with this particular
utility function,

01:00:30.360 --> 01:00:36.550 align:middle line:84%
we have the U double
prime divided by U prime,

01:00:36.550 --> 01:00:42.350 align:middle line:84%
those actually giving us
just the factor A here.

01:00:42.350 --> 01:00:47.080 align:middle line:84%
So we have our
price of the asset

01:00:47.080 --> 01:00:51.100 align:middle line:84%
should equal the
discounted expected cash

01:00:51.100 --> 01:00:53.306 align:middle line:90%
flow minus a risk adjustment.

01:00:53.306 --> 01:00:56.080 align:middle line:90%


01:00:56.080 --> 01:01:01.780 align:middle line:84%
And the risk adjustment
in the price,

01:01:01.780 --> 01:01:05.860 align:middle line:84%
that's like the fair
price for the asset,

01:01:05.860 --> 01:01:10.030 align:middle line:84%
varies depending upon
what the risk aversion

01:01:10.030 --> 01:01:12.350 align:middle line:90%
coefficient is of the agent.

01:01:12.350 --> 01:01:16.420 align:middle line:84%
And also the magnitude
of the shares

01:01:16.420 --> 01:01:20.830 align:middle line:84%
of the risky asset
that are obtained

01:01:20.830 --> 01:01:25.760 align:middle line:84%
and the variance of
the price outcome.

01:01:25.760 --> 01:01:32.710 align:middle line:84%
So the fair price
for the market agent

01:01:32.710 --> 01:01:36.460 align:middle line:84%
basically is balancing
all of these factors.

01:01:36.460 --> 01:01:39.650 align:middle line:90%
And let's see if--

01:01:39.650 --> 01:01:42.990 align:middle line:90%


01:01:42.990 --> 01:01:44.520 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:01:44.520 --> 01:01:49.440 align:middle line:84%
Well, with this result for a
single asset, what's really neat

01:01:49.440 --> 01:02:00.840 align:middle line:84%
is that we can generalize
from one asset to many

01:02:00.840 --> 01:02:06.240 align:middle line:84%
and set up the whole problem
with almost the same notation.

01:02:06.240 --> 01:02:12.570 align:middle line:84%
So we consider defining
a Q vector of shares

01:02:12.570 --> 01:02:16.800 align:middle line:90%
of assets 1 through n--

01:02:16.800 --> 01:02:23.370 align:middle line:84%
a1 through an-- and Qf units
of the risk-free asset.

01:02:23.370 --> 01:02:31.170 align:middle line:84%
And we can go through the same
logic of the initial wealth

01:02:31.170 --> 01:02:38.360 align:middle line:84%
being the Q shares per asset
times the price at time 0,

01:02:38.360 --> 01:02:45.230 align:middle line:84%
t0, of the assets plus the value
of the risk-free investment.

01:02:45.230 --> 01:02:52.520 align:middle line:84%
And we can assume that the
random prices at time t1

01:02:52.520 --> 01:02:55.466 align:middle line:84%
are jointly normally
distributed.

01:02:55.466 --> 01:02:59.330 align:middle line:90%


01:02:59.330 --> 01:03:07.370 align:middle line:84%
And so we then can think of
the market agent basically

01:03:07.370 --> 01:03:13.820 align:middle line:84%
adjusting the initial
shares Q by the X vector

01:03:13.820 --> 01:03:19.490 align:middle line:84%
and choosing the X vector to
maximize expected utility.

01:03:19.490 --> 01:03:27.860 align:middle line:84%
And we can just plug
everything in now

01:03:27.860 --> 01:03:36.520 align:middle line:84%
with vectors instead of scalars,
and the same arguments apply.

01:03:36.520 --> 01:03:38.890 align:middle line:84%
And we end up
getting, at the end

01:03:38.890 --> 01:03:43.330 align:middle line:84%
of the day, different
equilibrium prices

01:03:43.330 --> 01:03:48.460 align:middle line:84%
for the different assets
that are discounted

01:03:48.460 --> 01:03:55.870 align:middle line:84%
expected values of the
price plus a penalty

01:03:55.870 --> 01:04:03.940 align:middle line:84%
depending upon the risk
aversion of the agent.

01:04:03.940 --> 01:04:12.580 align:middle line:84%
And so we end up getting
this final result here.

01:04:12.580 --> 01:04:24.670 align:middle line:84%
Now, what's particularly
interesting from this setup

01:04:24.670 --> 01:04:30.930 align:middle line:84%
is to then say,
well, what prices

01:04:30.930 --> 01:04:39.510 align:middle line:84%
should be holding if this
market model is in equilibrium?

01:04:39.510 --> 01:04:46.030 align:middle line:84%
And so if the market
is in equilibrium,

01:04:46.030 --> 01:04:53.040 align:middle line:84%
then we should have expected
returns of risky assets

01:04:53.040 --> 01:05:00.150 align:middle line:90%
that satisfy this formula here.

01:05:00.150 --> 01:05:11.770 align:middle line:84%
And if we look at an initial
endowment of no shares at all,

01:05:11.770 --> 01:05:19.080 align:middle line:84%
and define the X allocation
to be an equilibrium

01:05:19.080 --> 01:05:25.350 align:middle line:84%
investment in the
market portfolio,

01:05:25.350 --> 01:05:32.100 align:middle line:84%
then our market
portfolio defined by X

01:05:32.100 --> 01:05:40.350 align:middle line:84%
will have a market value of f
tilde m, the sum of the Xi's

01:05:40.350 --> 01:05:43.410 align:middle line:90%
shares times f tilde i.

01:05:43.410 --> 01:05:47.940 align:middle line:84%
The price initially of
the market portfolio

01:05:47.940 --> 01:05:51.990 align:middle line:84%
will be just the
sum of the Xi shares

01:05:51.990 --> 01:05:56.070 align:middle line:90%
times the initial price as Pai.

01:05:56.070 --> 01:06:01.740 align:middle line:84%
And the return of
the market portfolio

01:06:01.740 --> 01:06:05.310 align:middle line:84%
will just be the
change in value divided

01:06:05.310 --> 01:06:09.240 align:middle line:84%
by the initial value,
which corresponds

01:06:09.240 --> 01:06:17.920 align:middle line:84%
to a weighted sum of the asset
changes or percentage returns.

01:06:17.920 --> 01:06:23.790 align:middle line:84%
So the percentage return r tilde
m is simply the weighted returns

01:06:23.790 --> 01:06:26.310 align:middle line:90%
of the individual assets.

01:06:26.310 --> 01:06:33.450 align:middle line:84%
And this is our expected
return of the market portfolio

01:06:33.450 --> 01:06:38.970 align:middle line:84%
when the underlying assets
satisfy these pricing

01:06:38.970 --> 01:06:40.680 align:middle line:90%
conditions.

01:06:40.680 --> 01:06:52.930 align:middle line:84%
Now, if we expand this expected
market return expression,

01:06:52.930 --> 01:07:04.260 align:middle line:84%
we can successively derive that
it equals the risk-free rate,

01:07:04.260 --> 01:07:14.100 align:middle line:84%
plus this blue factor times
the covariance of the initial w

01:07:14.100 --> 01:07:17.430 align:middle line:90%
vector with rm.

01:07:17.430 --> 01:07:33.800 align:middle line:84%
And this blue expression can be
solved for as the excess return

01:07:33.800 --> 01:07:39.830 align:middle line:84%
of the market portfolio divided
by the covariance term here.

01:07:39.830 --> 01:07:50.930 align:middle line:84%
And so we end up getting that
this covariance of w and rm

01:07:50.930 --> 01:07:57.710 align:middle line:84%
end up being simply pm
times the covariance of rm,

01:07:57.710 --> 01:07:59.870 align:middle line:90%
or the variance of rm.

01:07:59.870 --> 01:08:03.620 align:middle line:84%
And plugging this
in, we finally get

01:08:03.620 --> 01:08:10.010 align:middle line:84%
that the expected returns
of different assets

01:08:10.010 --> 01:08:16.700 align:middle line:84%
are simply a beta factor
times the excess return--

01:08:16.700 --> 01:08:20.300 align:middle line:84%
expected return of
the market portfolio

01:08:20.300 --> 01:08:22.340 align:middle line:90%
over the risk-free rate.

01:08:22.340 --> 01:08:27.490 align:middle line:84%
And so this
expression is actually

01:08:27.490 --> 01:08:33.130 align:middle line:84%
the expression for what's called
the security market line, where

01:08:33.130 --> 01:08:38.770 align:middle line:84%
we have the expected
return of asset i

01:08:38.770 --> 01:08:42.010 align:middle line:84%
is the risk-free rate,
plus a beta factor

01:08:42.010 --> 01:08:47.569 align:middle line:84%
times the excess return of the
market over the risk-free rate.

01:08:47.569 --> 01:08:53.080 align:middle line:84%
And this beta
factor is the ratio

01:08:53.080 --> 01:09:02.319 align:middle line:84%
of the covariance of asset i
and the market portfolio divided

01:09:02.319 --> 01:09:06.910 align:middle line:84%
by the variance of
the market portfolio.

01:09:06.910 --> 01:09:14.770 align:middle line:84%
And so this beta i is equivalent
to the regression coefficient

01:09:14.770 --> 01:09:19.280 align:middle line:84%
of the excess return of stocks
regressed on the excess return

01:09:19.280 --> 01:09:19.863 align:middle line:90%
of the market.

01:09:19.863 --> 01:09:24.060 align:middle line:90%


01:09:24.060 --> 01:09:31.290 align:middle line:84%
So in this model, we end up
having that different assets

01:09:31.290 --> 01:09:34.979 align:middle line:90%
will have different returns.

01:09:34.979 --> 01:09:43.390 align:middle line:84%
And those returns are
bounded below by rf,

01:09:43.390 --> 01:09:52.680 align:middle line:84%
but then are increased by having
a higher beta factor multiplying

01:09:52.680 --> 01:09:54.880 align:middle line:90%
the excess return of the market.

01:09:54.880 --> 01:09:58.920 align:middle line:84%
So you may be
familiar with issues

01:09:58.920 --> 01:10:04.470 align:middle line:84%
of betas in equity markets
and their being low betas

01:10:04.470 --> 01:10:09.000 align:middle line:90%
or high beta stocks.

01:10:09.000 --> 01:10:13.230 align:middle line:84%
In this model, we
have that the way

01:10:13.230 --> 01:10:18.990 align:middle line:84%
you get higher expected return
is to have a higher beta.

01:10:18.990 --> 01:10:30.410 align:middle line:84%
And so this also just
leads to understanding

01:10:30.410 --> 01:10:36.270 align:middle line:84%
what the equilibrium prices
are of individual assets.

01:10:36.270 --> 01:10:39.500 align:middle line:84%
If we take those
expressions for returns

01:10:39.500 --> 01:10:44.660 align:middle line:84%
and then reverse them into
expressions for the equilibrium

01:10:44.660 --> 01:10:49.520 align:middle line:84%
prices, we have that the
equilibrium prices are

01:10:49.520 --> 01:10:58.550 align:middle line:84%
the expected terminal
price divided by a discount

01:10:58.550 --> 01:11:03.500 align:middle line:84%
factor that discounts
by the discount

01:11:03.500 --> 01:11:06.330 align:middle line:84%
rate of the
risk-free investment,

01:11:06.330 --> 01:11:12.260 align:middle line:84%
but adding to that a penalty
based on the level of risk

01:11:12.260 --> 01:11:13.270 align:middle line:90%
that's being taken.

01:11:13.270 --> 01:11:16.640 align:middle line:90%


01:11:16.640 --> 01:11:22.120 align:middle line:84%
And here are just some
references on this topic

01:11:22.120 --> 01:11:27.760 align:middle line:90%
that you may want to check out.

01:11:27.760 --> 01:11:32.590 align:middle line:84%
The development
here was actually

01:11:32.590 --> 01:11:37.300 align:middle line:84%
taken from the first reference
by Frank de Jong and Barbara

01:11:37.300 --> 01:11:39.070 align:middle line:90%
Rindi.

01:11:39.070 --> 01:11:43.130 align:middle line:84%
There are other
important citations.

01:11:43.130 --> 01:11:47.240 align:middle line:84%
One of them that's of
interest is John Lintner,

01:11:47.240 --> 01:11:48.973 align:middle line:90%
"The Valuation of Risky Assets."

01:11:48.973 --> 01:11:51.520 align:middle line:90%


01:11:51.520 --> 01:11:58.690 align:middle line:84%
The capital asset pricing model
was actually formulated by John

01:11:58.690 --> 01:12:03.860 align:middle line:84%
Lintner and William Sharpe
back in the early '60s.

01:12:03.860 --> 01:12:09.310 align:middle line:84%
And I distributed one of
the articles of Lintner

01:12:09.310 --> 01:12:11.180 align:middle line:90%
in the Canvas site.

01:12:11.180 --> 01:12:16.900 align:middle line:84%
And it's actually nice to be
able to read a very technical

01:12:16.900 --> 01:12:21.630 align:middle line:90%
paper in an accessible way.

01:12:21.630 --> 01:12:25.570 align:middle line:84%
And so I think that you'll
enjoy checking that paper out.

01:12:25.570 --> 01:12:28.460 align:middle line:90%


01:12:28.460 --> 01:12:30.680 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:12:30.680 --> 01:12:37.820 align:middle line:84%
Well, let me turn then to the
main topic in our syllabus

01:12:37.820 --> 01:12:42.740 align:middle line:84%
for today, but it's
the tail end of today,

01:12:42.740 --> 01:12:47.850 align:middle line:84%
to talk about stochastic
processes, concepts.

01:12:47.850 --> 01:12:53.450 align:middle line:84%
And one of the most important
concepts in stochastic processes

01:12:53.450 --> 01:12:59.280 align:middle line:84%
is the concept of a
Martingale process.

01:12:59.280 --> 01:13:06.710 align:middle line:84%
And so if we have a
stochastic process given

01:13:06.710 --> 01:13:12.410 align:middle line:84%
by a sequence of
random variables Xn,

01:13:12.410 --> 01:13:19.450 align:middle line:84%
we can define a
related process Mn,

01:13:19.450 --> 01:13:28.480 align:middle line:84%
which is defined as functions
of the first n X's for each n.

01:13:28.480 --> 01:13:36.790 align:middle line:84%
So if we have Mn depending
only on the first nX's

01:13:36.790 --> 01:13:41.330 align:middle line:84%
in some well-defined way
given by a function fn,

01:13:41.330 --> 01:13:48.260 align:middle line:84%
then we will say that this
Mn sequence, which is random,

01:13:48.260 --> 01:13:50.630 align:middle line:84%
because of its
dependence on the X's,

01:13:50.630 --> 01:14:00.100 align:middle line:84%
it will be a Martingale if the
expected value of the n-th M,

01:14:00.100 --> 01:14:05.620 align:middle line:84%
given the first n
minus 1 X's, is simply

01:14:05.620 --> 01:14:10.780 align:middle line:84%
equal to the n minus
first value of M.

01:14:10.780 --> 01:14:15.970 align:middle line:84%
And we also are going to require
that the magnitude of the Mn's

01:14:15.970 --> 01:14:20.280 align:middle line:84%
as a finite expectation
for all n too.

01:14:20.280 --> 01:14:25.830 align:middle line:84%
Now, let's consider perhaps
the simplest example

01:14:25.830 --> 01:14:30.640 align:middle line:84%
of a Martingale, which is
basically a random walk.

01:14:30.640 --> 01:14:36.390 align:middle line:84%
Suppose the Xn sequence is just
independent random variables

01:14:36.390 --> 01:14:47.280 align:middle line:84%
that each has mean 0, and Sn
is the sum of the first nX's.

01:14:47.280 --> 01:14:53.940 align:middle line:84%
Then this Sn is a random
walk with steps given by X.

01:14:53.940 --> 01:14:56.940 align:middle line:84%
And it's going to be a
Martingale with respect

01:14:56.940 --> 01:15:01.680 align:middle line:90%
to the original process of Xn's.

01:15:01.680 --> 01:15:04.770 align:middle line:84%
Now, we can make this
example, even simpler

01:15:04.770 --> 01:15:09.750 align:middle line:84%
by assuming that the X's are
independent and identically

01:15:09.750 --> 01:15:13.620 align:middle line:84%
distributed, and
maybe that they're

01:15:13.620 --> 01:15:18.590 align:middle line:84%
discrete with just a plus
1 or minus 1 step increment

01:15:18.590 --> 01:15:21.320 align:middle line:90%
with equal probability.

01:15:21.320 --> 01:15:29.870 align:middle line:84%
So these X's have expectation
equal to 0 and variance equal

01:15:29.870 --> 01:15:31.220 align:middle line:90%
to 1.

01:15:31.220 --> 01:15:36.180 align:middle line:84%
And the process Sn
has expectation 0,

01:15:36.180 --> 01:15:37.910 align:middle line:90%
the sum of the expectations.

01:15:37.910 --> 01:15:41.540 align:middle line:84%
And the variance of
Sn is the variance

01:15:41.540 --> 01:15:46.260 align:middle line:84%
of a sum of independent
steps, increments.

01:15:46.260 --> 01:15:48.840 align:middle line:84%
And so it's just the
sum of the variances.

01:15:48.840 --> 01:15:50.840 align:middle line:90%
So that's n.

01:15:50.840 --> 01:15:55.970 align:middle line:84%
Well, here's a random
walk realization

01:15:55.970 --> 01:16:00.380 align:middle line:90%
of this model with 100 steps.

01:16:00.380 --> 01:16:05.880 align:middle line:84%
And so it basically
starts at time 0,

01:16:05.880 --> 01:16:08.630 align:middle line:84%
and then as steps
occur, increments

01:16:08.630 --> 01:16:11.960 align:middle line:90%
up or down plus or minus 1.

01:16:11.960 --> 01:16:16.880 align:middle line:84%
If we increase from
100 steps to 1,000,

01:16:16.880 --> 01:16:22.610 align:middle line:84%
this is what a
realization looks like.

01:16:22.610 --> 01:16:27.100 align:middle line:84%
And then here's an extension
from 1,000 steps to 10,000

01:16:27.100 --> 01:16:28.160 align:middle line:90%
steps.

01:16:28.160 --> 01:16:33.610 align:middle line:84%
And what's rather significant
is the general shape

01:16:33.610 --> 01:16:37.880 align:middle line:84%
of this random walk process as
we increase the number of steps.

01:16:37.880 --> 01:16:43.090 align:middle line:84%
It becomes this
very jagged path.

01:16:43.090 --> 01:16:48.850 align:middle line:84%
And it actually corresponds
to Brownian motion models

01:16:48.850 --> 01:16:50.660 align:middle line:90%
that we'll come across.

01:16:50.660 --> 01:16:56.560 align:middle line:84%
Now, if we were to realize
10 different paths of length

01:16:56.560 --> 01:17:02.390 align:middle line:84%
10,000, here are
examples of that.

01:17:02.390 --> 01:17:08.000 align:middle line:84%
And so one can see that
for low number of steps

01:17:08.000 --> 01:17:11.320 align:middle line:84%
there's not very much
variability that increases as we

01:17:11.320 --> 01:17:13.600 align:middle line:90%
increase the number of steps.

01:17:13.600 --> 01:17:18.540 align:middle line:84%
And here, we're
just drawing bands

01:17:18.540 --> 01:17:22.410 align:middle line:84%
at plus or minus multiples
of the standard deviation

01:17:22.410 --> 01:17:25.830 align:middle line:90%
of the random walk value.

01:17:25.830 --> 01:17:34.110 align:middle line:84%
And so these standard
deviation levels basically

01:17:34.110 --> 01:17:37.680 align:middle line:84%
apply just through
the mathematics

01:17:37.680 --> 01:17:44.010 align:middle line:84%
of calculating one or two
standard deviations of the sum.

01:17:44.010 --> 01:17:52.230 align:middle line:84%
And what we know from
our central limit theorem

01:17:52.230 --> 01:17:56.820 align:middle line:84%
is that the distribution of
possible values at any given

01:17:56.820 --> 01:18:00.060 align:middle line:84%
time step will be
closely modeled

01:18:00.060 --> 01:18:03.825 align:middle line:84%
by a Gaussian distribution
because of the central limit

01:18:03.825 --> 01:18:04.325 align:middle line:90%
theorem.

01:18:04.325 --> 01:18:06.240 align:middle line:90%
[BELL RINGING]

01:18:06.240 --> 01:18:07.240 align:middle line:90%
We have a stop.

01:18:07.240 --> 01:18:09.540 align:middle line:84%
OK, I'm going to take
two more minutes just

01:18:09.540 --> 01:18:12.350 align:middle line:84%
to show you a more--
not a more interesting,

01:18:12.350 --> 01:18:16.090 align:middle line:84%
but a related but very
interesting Martingale.

01:18:16.090 --> 01:18:23.710 align:middle line:84%
Suppose that once we've
calculated this random walk Sn,

01:18:23.710 --> 01:18:30.760 align:middle line:84%
we look at the square of Sn
and subtract n sigma squared.

01:18:30.760 --> 01:18:35.890 align:middle line:84%
It turns out that this
is a martingale as well.

01:18:35.890 --> 01:18:42.490 align:middle line:84%
And here's one realization
over 10,000 steps,

01:18:42.490 --> 01:18:46.450 align:middle line:90%
and here are 10 realizations.

01:18:46.450 --> 01:18:49.210 align:middle line:84%
And so these look
very, very different

01:18:49.210 --> 01:18:52.190 align:middle line:90%
from the simple random walk.

01:18:52.190 --> 01:18:55.300 align:middle line:84%
But these, as well,
are martingales.

01:18:55.300 --> 01:18:59.530 align:middle line:84%
And so one of the--
what we'll see next time

01:18:59.530 --> 01:19:07.480 align:middle line:84%
is how martingales have very
special properties which

01:19:07.480 --> 01:19:13.150 align:middle line:84%
can be used to solve challenging
probability problems.

01:19:13.150 --> 01:19:19.500 align:middle line:84%
And just as a closing remark,
does anyone here know where

01:19:19.500 --> 01:19:21.425 align:middle line:84%
the term "martingale"
comes from?

01:19:21.425 --> 01:19:24.750 align:middle line:90%


01:19:24.750 --> 01:19:27.870 align:middle line:90%
Do we have any equestrians?

01:19:27.870 --> 01:19:31.590 align:middle line:84%
There's a bridle and a horse
called a martingale bridle

01:19:31.590 --> 01:19:36.250 align:middle line:84%
that keeps the horse
straight, looking straight.

01:19:36.250 --> 01:19:41.010 align:middle line:84%
So it is a bridle
that has pressure

01:19:41.010 --> 01:19:44.050 align:middle line:84%
on both sides of the mouth to
keep the horse looking straight.

01:19:44.050 --> 01:19:45.720 align:middle line:84%
And what's true
about a martingale

01:19:45.720 --> 01:19:48.720 align:middle line:84%
is that the expected
value in the future

01:19:48.720 --> 01:19:54.910 align:middle line:84%
is always a constant level equal
to the last level of the series.

01:19:54.910 --> 01:20:00.550 align:middle line:84%
So that property will end up
being very, very convenient,

01:20:00.550 --> 01:20:04.610 align:middle line:84%
and we'll see examples
of that next time.

01:20:04.610 --> 01:20:10.000 align:middle line:90%